window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["2e"]={"https://theconversation.com/eurovision-2027-is-canada-singing-its-way-into-the-european-union-295040":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"f5a3b621e4ca9405d1351ccd07a08a3d847b4bdda0afe4e1bccdad82bf61a540","read":"https://theconversation.com/eurovision-2027-is-canada-singing-its-way-into-the-european-union-295040","bytes":185836,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Eurovision 2027: Is Canada singing its way into the European Union?","by":[{"n":"Petra Dolata","r":"Associate Professor, History, University of Calgary"}],"pub":"2026-10-28T10:55:49Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Officials are meeting this week for the ",["21st Canada-European Union Leaders’ summit","https://www.pm.gc.ca/en/news/news-releases/2026/10/25/prime-minister-carney-host-canada-european-union-summit-montreal"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["It comes only a month after European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen ",["offered Canada associate membership","https://commission.europa.eu/strategy-and-policy/state-union_en"],". ",["A draft statement from Montréal","https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/european-union-canada-associate-member-9.7399578"],", where Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney, von der Leyen and EU Council President António Costa met for two days to discuss strengthening ties, calls for an “ever closer association” that will result “in a more active foreign and security policy partnership.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["The talks ",["follow a series","https://www.international.gc.ca/world-monde/international_relations-relations_internationales/eu-ue/agreement-accord.aspx?lang=eng"]," of ",["milestones","https://www.pm.gc.ca/en/news/news-releases/2026/04/28/prime-minister-carney-represent-canada-first-time-european-political"]," in the relationship between the transatlantic partners, including the ",["announcement in July","https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/jul/01/canada-joins-eurovision-song-contest"]," that Canada was joining the Eurovision song contest. It’s the first country to do so since Australia entered in 2015."]},{"k":"p","s":["Eurovision is held every year in May, with member countries submitting one original song. Over the years, the event’s format has changed from an exclusive jury vote to a viewer televote and today’s combination of both."]},{"k":"p","s":["Sometimes, voting ",["can become political","https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/eurovision-canada-politics-soft-power-1.6841812"],", with viewers voting according to their ",["geopolitical preferences","https://www.chathamhouse.org/publications/the-world-today/2023-04/eurovision-war-and-geopolitics-pop"]," or diaspora communities casting ballots for their former home countries regardless of the quality of the performance."]},{"k":"p","s":["This may be good news for Canada, which is currently basking in European affection. The current ",["geopolitical romance","https://www.policymagazine.ca/the-canada-eu-romance-friends-with-benefits-or-cautionary-fairy-tale/"]," between Canada and the EU could help a Canadian song win. It wouldn’t be the first Canadian win, though. ",["Céline Dion took home the prize in 1988","https://ici.radio-canada.ca/rci/en/news/2165332/celine-dion-cheers-on-eurovision-contest-as-fans-hope-for-surprise-performance-at-final"],", representing Switzerland."]},{"k":"h","s":["Uranium fears in the 1950s"]},{"k":"p","s":["The EU and Canada are clearly getting closer. But will historians look back at Eurovision and other developments as the beginning of comprehensive Canada-EU integration? Probably not — and history shows why."]},{"k":"p","s":["A look at the history of Canada-EU relations reveals a shared interest in deeper integration that has rarely materialized into anything concrete."]},{"k":"p","s":["More than half a century ago, a couple of years after the 1957 ",["Treaty of Rome","https://www.europarl.europa.eu/about-parliament/en/in-the-past/the-parliament-and-the-treaties/treaty-of-rome"]," had established the ",["European Economic Community","https://www.econlib.org/library/Enc/EuropeanEconomicCommunity.html"]," and ",["the European Atomic Energy Community","https://www.europarl.europa.eu/about-parliament/en/in-the-past/the-parliament-and-the-treaties/euratom-treaty"],", Canada was the first industrialized country to sign an agreement with the latter organization. In October 1959, Canada became a signatory to the ",["Peaceful Uses of Atomic Energy","https://carleton.ca/ces/eulearning/economics/ceta/canada-and-the-eu-an-overview/"]," deal."]},{"k":"p","s":["At the time, Canada was worried about losing the United States as a major export market for its uranium. That’s because the U.S. had only agreed to a supply contract until 1962, expecting much lower demand for Canadian uranium after that."]},{"k":"p","s":["Prime Minister John Diefenbaker, who wanted to diversify Canada’s trade relations and revive imperial links with the United Kingdom, ",["looked to Europe for future uranium markets","https://www.commonwealthroundtable.co.uk/commonwealth/americas/canada/canadian-concerns-of-a-different-kind-of-brexit-britains-first-application-to-the-eec-and-canadas-commonwealth-appeal/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["He added Germany and Italy to the list of countries to buy Canadian uranium. Canada also tried to ensure these new customers would only use the uranium for peaceful purposes."]},{"k":"p","s":["To do so, Canada considered an agreement with the European Atomic Energy Community, of which both West Germany and Italy were member states, as the best option. For its part, the organization believed the agreement with Canada not only provided access to Canadian uranium and civilian nuclear technology but also gave it international visibility."]},{"k":"p","s":["But in retrospect, beyond scientific collaboration, not much else was achieved. Canada had already lost the race to the U.S., whose aggressive export policy led to Western Europe adopting American nuclear technology."]},{"k":"h","s":["Nixon-Trudeau tensions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Diversification away from the U.S. was also behind the ",["1976 framework agreement for commercial and economic co-operation","https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/PDF/?uri=CELEX:21976A0706(01)&qid=1793021640385"],", which Canada signed as the first industrialized country with the ",["European Community","https://www.britannica.com/topic/European-Community-European-economic-association"],", which later became the European Union."]},{"k":"p","s":["Canada was surprised by U.S. President ",["Richard Nixon’s 1971 announcement of a new economic program","https://doi.org/10.1111/ehr.13052"]," that abolished the conversion of gold to American dollars and terminated fixed exchange rates."]},{"k":"p","s":["Canada hadn’t been warned about the immediate imposition of a unilateral 10 per cent surcharge on imports, and it wasn’t exempted. In response to these Nixon shocks and fuelled by long-simmering anxieties about Canadian independence, ",["the Liberal government under Pierre Trudeau proposed a third option","https://doi.org/10.1080/02722010409481207"]," to reduce dependence on the U.S. and actively seek new partnerships, mostly with Europe."]},{"k":"p","s":["As they did in the late 1950s, the Europeans were receptive to Canadian advances because they wanted ",["access to Canada’s energy resources","https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-43250-8_55"],". This was especially true in the wake of the 1973 oil crisis, which had not only caused Western European countries to desperately search for oil supplies outside the Middle East and the Soviet Union, but also led to ",["their heightened demands for uranium","https://doi.org/10.1080/07036338108428800"]," as nuclear power programs ramped up to provide alternative energy."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the framework agreement between Canada and European countries never realized its full potential. Instead, ",["Canada deepened its relations with the U.S. and signed a free-trade agreement in the 1980s","https://doi.org/10.3138/9781442689541"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Can momentum be sustained?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Today, Canada faces another trade crisis with the U.S. Canadian oil and gas is in high demand, even in a decarbonizing Europe that’s cut off from Russian supplies."]},{"k":"p","s":["In addition, the ",["current nuclear renaissance","https://www.cbc.ca/radio/frontburner/carney-nuclear-renaissance-9.7296011"],", the combined result of climate change action and fossil fuel geopolitics, has made Canada’s uranium a highly sought-after resource."]},{"k":"p","s":["No wonder the EU and Canada are seemingly moving closer. But will the rhetoric around ",["middle power solidarity","https://theconversation.com/mark-carneys-davos-speech-marks-a-major-departure-from-canadas-usual-approach-to-the-u-s-274090"]," and common values be enough to sustain the momentum?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Europeans and Canadians have always seen each other as friends, and Canada’s Eurovision participation will only cement that war relationship. But historically, closer ties have not led to deeper integration in the long run."]}],"chars":5602},"https://theconversation.com/friday-essay-to-tackle-the-dreadful-rise-in-antisemitism-we-must-speak-the-truth-about-israels-war-crimes-296263":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:47Z","sha":"ac8e29a747e97f91072af8cc5669acda8ea544a148050af03aacad7607794230","read":"https://theconversation.com/friday-essay-to-tackle-the-dreadful-rise-in-antisemitism-we-must-speak-the-truth-about-israels-war-crimes-296263","bytes":208563,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Friday essay: to tackle the dreadful rise in antisemitism, we must speak the truth about Israel’s war crimes","by":[{"n":"Robert Manne","r":"Emeritus Professor of Politics, La Trobe University"}],"pub":"2026-11-12T19:03:45Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Over the past weeks I have watched with interest the ways, following the horrific Hamas-led massacre of 1,200 Israelis on ",["October 7 2023","https://theconversation.com/three-years-after-october-7-global-attention-on-gaza-has-receded-the-real-test-will-come-after-israels-election-292360"],", prominent Jews with a connection to Australia have justified, in response, the killing by the Israel Defence Forces, or IDF, of around 75,000 Gazan Palestinians, the majority of whom were innocent civilians, or ",["more than 20,000","https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/06/1167790"]," babies or children."]},{"k":"p","s":["Recently, the British historian, Lord ",["Andrew Roberts","https://www.andrew-roberts.net/"],", was interviewed by Geraldine Doogue on the ABC Radio National program ",["Global Roaming","https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/global-roaming/lord-andrew-roberts-on-bondi-oct-7/107109506"],". According to Lord Roberts, the massacre of the Israeli Jews by Hamas and ",["Islamic Jihad","https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/sanctions/1267/aq_sanctions_list/summaries/entity/islamic-jihad-group"]," was an unspeakable moral crime, while the killing by the IDF of perhaps 40 or 50 times as many innocent Palestinians, was the kind of thing that, alas, happens in fighting a just war."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even the propaganda department of the IDF has barely challenged the estimates of the numbers of civilian deaths. However, without pointing to any evidence, Roberts claimed the number of innocent Palestinians massacred by the IDF had been greatly exaggerated."]},{"k":"p","s":["What was more interesting, however, was Roberts’ contrast between the killing of the Jews and Palestinians since the massacre of October 7. According to Roberts, the October 7 Hamas killers were in a “frenzy”. The things they did were “foul”, “vicious” and “disgusting”, resembling the accounts of warfare during “medieval” times and in recent history of the Japanese at “the ",["rape of Nanking","https://www.britannica.com/event/Nanjing-Massacre"],"”. With “civilised countries”, this kind of “frenzied killing” did not take place."]},{"k":"p","s":["In Gaza, the IDF’s killings were “totally different you see”. He said:"]},{"k":"p","s":["At no point did the Israelis look into the eyes of Palestinians and murder their children after torturing and raping them […] It’s an entirely different kind of warfare. You get tanks of course and aeroplanes […] Also of course you’ve got to kill as many terrorists as possible […] They [Hamas] also don’t care how many of their population die. In fact, in terms of public relations the more that die the better […] As an historian I try to stay rational and logical […] I see a totally different thing between the face-to-face massacre after torture and rape of Israelis on the 7th of October and the warfare that took place in Gaza between the IDF and Hamas."]},{"k":"p","s":["The IDF’s post-October 7 destruction in Gaza involved anything but conventional warfare. Anyone who has followed the Israeli campaign knows the Palestinians were killed according to an AI program called “Lavender”. It was first revealed in an article in ",["the 972 Magazine","https://www.972mag.com/lavender-ai-israeli-army-gaza/"]," on April 3 2024, written by Yuval Abraham, one of the filmmakers of the documentary ",["NAZA","https://theconversation.com/naza-harrowing-documentary-shows-how-killing-in-gaza-became-just-another-days-work-293710"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["When NAZA was shown at the Venice Film Festival, ",["the applause lasted","https://www.youtube.com/shorts/D-mnu76-tEQ"]," for 24 minutes. Israel had complete control of the air. The number of Palestinians who could be taken out as “collateral damage” – 20, 50, even 100 – depended on the seniority of the member of Hamas the IDF intelligence believed was sleeping in the same building."]},{"k":"p","s":["If Roberts had thought for five minutes about his black-and-white contrast between the Hamas killing of Israelis and the IDF killings of Palestinians in the most recent war in Gaza, it might have occurred to him that in the Holocaust, which Roberts almost certainly would regard as the darkest crime in the history of humankind, the Germans did not kill most of the six million Jews in a “frenzy” or in “face-to-face” combat\"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Eventually, the SS leadership recognised the most efficient and orderly method of exterminating the Jews was with deception. Vast numbers of the Jews who were murdered in the Holocaust were led naked into the gas chambers of the extermination camps, believing they were about to be showered. (Or in the case of my maternal grandfather, into a sealed truck in ",["Chelmno","https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/chelmno"],".)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Does Roberts believe this was a more civilised way of killing Jews than, for example, the often frenzied face-to-face pogroms that broke out during the ",["Russian Civil War","https://www.britannica.com/event/Russian-Civil-War"],", where tens of thousands of Jews were murdered?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Any judgement about the comparative morality of Hamas’ frenzied, hate-filled killing of Jews on October 7 and Israel’s nonchalant, formulaic, algorithmic killing of innocent Palestinians in response, is to my mind dauntingly difficult. Not so, apparently, for Lord Roberts."]},{"k":"h","s":["‘Jew hatred’ and genocide"]},{"k":"p","s":["In late October, Schwartz Media, owned by Morry Schwartz, published a Jewish Quarterly essay (edited by Jonathan Pearlman), ",["Jew-Hatred: The Latest Episode","https://jewishquarterly.com/essay/2026/10/jew-hatred"],", by the distinguished British historian, Sir Simon Schama. One of Schama’s contentions is that the claim the IDF has committed genocide against the Palestinians is “the most consequential” contemporary expression of millennia-old Jew-hatred."]},{"k":"p","s":["Almost everything Schama says about the crime of genocide is false."]},{"k":"p","s":["According to Schama, genocide is “the organised, systematic extermination of an entire population; a ",["Wannsee","https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/wannsee-conference-and-the-final-solution"]," -type, planned mass murder”. Schama seems not to recognise that largely because of the passionate political struggle of ",["Raphael Lemkin","https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/coining-a-word-and-championing-a-cause-the-story-of-raphael-lemkin"],", in 1948, genocide became a crime under international law."]},{"k":"p","s":["When an international court will decide whether or not Israel is guilty of genocide against the Palestinians in Gaza, it will do so not according to Schama’s Holocaust-inflected private definition, but according to law – the ",["Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide","https://www.ohchr.org/en/instruments-mechanisms/instruments/convention-prevention-and-punishment-crime-genocide"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Convention outlines what the International Commission of Jurists describes as “five specific underlying acts, any one of which may be constitutive of the crime of genocide”."]},{"k":"p","s":["These are: “killing members of a national, ethnical, racial or religious group”; “causing serious bodily or mental harm to members of the group”; “deliberately inflicting on the group conditions of life calculated to bring about its physical destruction in whole or in part; \"imposing measures to prevent births within the group”; or “forcibly transferring children of the group to another group”."]},{"k":"p","s":["When the genocide case is heard before an international court, one of the most important questions will be Israel’s intent. Here is all Schama says on this vital question about Israel’s arguably genocidal post October 7 massacres."]},{"k":"p","s":["Did Israel’s Minister of Defense, Yoav Gallant, not call Palestinians “human animals” and wish out loud for their extermination?"]},{"k":"p","s":["No, he did not. On 9 October 2023, as details of unspeakable atrocities were becoming known, Gallant used the phrase when speaking of the Hamas terrorists who had planned and executed the horrific massacre, whose power — and if necessary whose lives — he wished to see ended. Therein lies the difference between war and genocide."]},{"k":"p","s":["What ",["Gallant actually said","https://www.icj.org/resource-region/middle-east-and-north-africa/palestine/"]," was quite different:"]},{"k":"p","s":["I have ordered a complete siege on the Gaza Strip. There will be no electricity, no food, no water, no fuel, everything is closed. We are fighting human animals and we act accordingly."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under conditions of total siege, the principal victims will, of course, be the civilian population of Gaza. Without food and water, everyone suffers, eventually everyone dies. The Gazan Palestinians, not just the Hamas terrorists, are Gallant’s “human animals”. One day later, to underline the point, Gallant promised: “Gaza won’t return to what it was before. We will eliminate everything.” Not Hamas; everything."]},{"k":"p","s":["More importantly, as all those who write about the events in Gaza should know, when South Africa ",["outlined its case","https://www.icj-cij.org/sites/default/files/case-related/192/192-20231228-app-01-00-en.pdf"]," to the International Court of Justice about Israel’s commission of genocide, it devoted nine tight, carefully footnoted pages to the question of Israel’s genocidal intent regarding the Palestinians in Gaza. What follows is a small sample."]},{"k":"p","s":["Time and again, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu described the coming battle between the Jews and the Palestinians as a war between light and darkness, civilisation and barbarism, good and evil. As Israel prepared for its ground invasion, Netanyahu called upon Israeli troops to “remember Amalek”. In the Torah, as the invasion of Amalek territory begins, the Jews are commanded by God: “Spare no one, but kill alike men and women, infants and sucklings.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["On October 12, the president of Israel, Isaac Herzog, made clear that “the entire nation” of the Gazan Palestinians was responsible for what occurred on October 7. And that the nation’s “backbone” must be “broken” as a result."]},{"k":"p","s":["Three days earlier, the Coordinator of Government Activity in the Territories, Major General Ghassan Alian, had warned: “Human animals are dealt with accordingly. Israel has imposed a total blockade on Gaza, no electricity, no water […] You wanted hell, you will get hell.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["On October 11, Ezra Yachin, a 95-year-old veteran of the notorious and brutal 1948 massacre of Palestinians at ",["Deir Yassin","https://www.britannica.com/place/Deir-Yassin"],", was wheeled out. He broadcast on social media: “Erase the memory of them [the Gazan Palestinians]. Erase them, their families, mothers and children. These animals can no longer live.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["On October 13, the Minister for Energy and Infrastructure, Israel Katz, tweeted: “All the civilian population in Gaza […] will not receive a drop of water or a single battery until they leave the world.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["On November 1, the Minister for Heritage, Amichai Eliyahu, described the north of Gaza as “more beautiful than ever. Everything is blown up and flattened, simply a pleasure for the eyes.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["On November 11, the Minister for Agriculture, Avi Dichter, described what was happening as “the Gaza Nakba”. ",["Nakba","https://theconversation.com/the-nakba-75-years-after-losing-their-home-the-palestinians-are-still-experiencing-the-catastrophe-205413"],", the catastrophe, is the Palestinian name for the killings and the expulsions of 700,000 Palestinians at the time of the Arab–Jewish War of 1948."]},{"k":"p","s":["On December 25, a former ",["Knesset","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Knesset"]," member was reported thus: “I tell you, in Gaza without exception, they are all terrorists, sons of dogs. They must be exterminated, all of them killed.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["I hope not to be misunderstood. I have no idea whether the evidence collected by the South Africans or by others will eventually convince an international court of Israel’s commission of genocide in Gaza. I do know, however, that Schama’s suggestion that the “human animals” remark of Yoav Gallant was restricted to Hamas terrorists and did not involve the entire Palestinian population in Gaza, and that it was the sole or principal piece of evidence of Israeli post October 7 genocidal intent, are both false."]},{"k":"p","s":["Towards the end of his essay, Schama writes:"]},{"k":"p","s":["So, for all the chants, this paroxysm of rage is, at its psychological core, not about Palestine, Israel or even the Jews. The hatred is about the haters: a failure of self-recognition: a displacement of the obligation to make amends. The Nazification of Israel […] act[s] as a reprieve from failing to do much about saving the Jewish victims even after the facts of their annihilation [in the Holocaust] were known. Likewise framing the Jews as history’s paradigmatic imperialist oppressors spares the descendants of actual colonisers from too much critical self-examination. Put the Jews in the dock rather than your great-grandparents."]},{"k":"p","s":["I do not know how much Schama knows about Australia. However, the idea that the group Schama calls “white progressives”, including Australians, are trying to hide the guilt they ought to feel about the Indigenous dispossession in Australia, by defaming Israel for its supposedly genocidal campaign in Gaza, is about as comprehensive a misunderstanding of the politics of Gaza in this country as it is possible to imagine."]},{"k":"p","s":["Schama argues, repeatedly, that those who accuse Israel of genocide in Gaza know nothing of the Holocaust. I am not sure how he can also, absurdly and contradicting himself, claim the genocide accusers are displacing onto Israel the guilt they ought to feel about their country’s failure to save the Jews during the Holocaust, of whose history they are supposedly ignorant."]},{"k":"p","s":["More importantly, the hundreds of thousands of Australians who have demonstrated for the Gazan Palestinians – for the murdered, the wounded, the surviving two million whose lives lie in ruins – are overwhelmingly the same people who lead the struggle in Australia for Indigenous rights and the recognition of the tragedy of Indigenous dispossession."]},{"k":"p","s":["At the very head of those who march and chant for the Palestinians against what they believe to be Israel’s genocide in Gaza are Australia’s younger Indigenous leaders."]},{"k":"p","s":["At the conclusion of his denigration of the people who have reacted with appropriate horror at Israel’s pitiless destruction of Gaza, Schama thinks he can still speak with kindness, even intimacy, to the Palestinian people. “[S]hould we, Jews and non-Jews, Israelis and Palestinians, fail to listen to each other with respectful attention, informed by the truth, the unhappy victims of such deafness will, in the end, be all of us.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["A better title for Jew-Hatred would be Chutzpah."]},{"k":"h","s":["Jillian Segal’s ‘astonishing overreach’"]},{"k":"p","s":["In October, Jillian Segal AO, Australia’s special envoy to combat antisemitism, published ",["her progress report","https://www.aseca.gov.au/sites/default/files/2026-10/combatting-antisemitism-special-envoys-priorities.pdf"]," into the curse of rising antisemitism in Australia. The content has been expertly summarised ",["in Deepcut News","https://www.deepcutnews.com/p/its-time-to-sack-jillian-segal-jeff"]," by Jeffrey Loewenstein, a former chairman of the Anti-Defamation Commission in Australia."]},{"k":"p","s":["“If Segal has her way, nearly every facet of life in Australia would, in effect, be subject to vetting by a cohort of pro-Israel supporters who wish for us to be muzzled as Israel carries out the worst crimes imaginable,” he wrote."]},{"k":"p","s":["“The unelected Segal wants to reshape the arts, vet our public broadcasters, oversee the use of venues, stages, art spaces, what festivals should and should not be doing, revise our visa programs, sit atop digital governance and online regulation and tell universities how to conduct themselves. The gall of the overreach is extraordinary and yet mainstream media freely aired Segal’s report and arguments with barely a care for its implications on Australian democracy.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Beyond this astonishing overreach, what emerges from Segal is an even more alarming dimension of her progress report."]},{"k":"p","s":["On 14 occasions, Segal recommends the adoption throughout Australian institutions of the International Holocaust Remembrance Alliance’s ",["definition of antisemitism","https://holocaustremembrance.com/resources/working-definition-antisemitism"],". The reason seems clear. Despite acknowledging not every criticism of Israel or its government is antisemitic, time and again the IHRA definition links criticism of Israel and antisemitism."]},{"k":"p","s":["According to IHRA, it is antisemitic to claim that Israel exaggerates the Holocaust. It is antisemitic to accuse Jews of being more loyal to Israel than to their own homelands. It is antisemitic to deny Jews their right to self-determination or to describe Israel “as a racist endeavour”. It is antisemitic to compare Israel’s policies to those of the Nazis. And it is antisemitic to hold Jews in the diaspora responsible for Israel’s actions."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under the IHRA definition, everyone in Australia who criticises Israel’s policies in Gaza and the West Bank, or the diaspora for its uncritical support of those policies, will have to be very careful they have not strayed into territory the IHRA definition has forbidden and become vulnerable to the charge of antisemitism."]},{"k":"p","s":["An addition to the Segal agenda in her recent progress report is the claim the most important camp of antisemitism in Australia is to be found among left-wing progressives:"]},{"k":"p","s":["Modern, progressive antisemitism is the most pervasive manifestation of antisemitism facing Australia today […] Modern progressive antisemitism has grown out of, and is especially prevalent in, some institutions of intellectual and cultural leadership, including universities, media, arts organisations and human rights NGOs […] The claims that Israel has committed humanity’s worst crimes — including genocide, ethnic cleansing and apartheid — allow for the depiction of anyone who associates or aligns with Israel as complicit in its purported evil."]},{"k":"p","s":["As it happens, Segal has supplied us, by implication, with plentiful examples of what she takes to be modern, progressive antisemitism in Australia and elsewhere."]},{"k":"p","s":["According to Segal, anyone who claims Israel has committed genocide in Gaza, as do very many international human rights bodies and genocide scholars, is an antisemite. According to Segal, anyone who believes it is almost self-evident that by destroying the houses, hospitals, schools, universities, mosques, cultural sites, agricultural lands, etc., Israel has been conducting a ruthless policy of ethnic cleansing in Gaza, thwarted only by the failure of Israel to find a country willing to take the two million survivors, is an antisemite."]},{"k":"p","s":["According to Segal, anyone who argues that by turning a blind eye to the daily attacks on the Palestinian villagers by the Jewish settlers, Israel has begun upon a policy of ethnic cleansing in the West Bank, is an antisemite. And according to Segal, anyone who argues the combination of liberal and democratic rights for the citizens of Israel and the suppression of those rights for the Palestinians in the West Bank (and now also most of Gaza) resembles the former system of apartheid in South Africa, is an antisemite."]},{"k":"p","s":["By labelling as antisemitism the opinions of a goodly part of the Australian left, and beyond that the politically informed public, the Segal report represents the most insidious attack on political free speech in the recent history of Australia."]},{"k":"h","s":["Asking to be left alone"]},{"k":"p","s":["Throughout my life, I have been preoccupied by the evil of antisemitism. One of my books, ",["The Culture of Forgetting: Helen Demidenko and the Holocaust","https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/14060105-the-culture-of-forgetting"],", was about Helen “Demidenko’s” celebrated novel, ",["The Hand that Signed the Paper","https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/36258559-the-hand-that-signed-the-paper"],". The novel revived the key Nazi concept that drove the Holocaust: Bolshevism and Stalinism as the expression of Jewish political power."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another of my books, ",["The Mind of the Islamic State","https://www.blackincbooks.com.au/news/discussing-dangerous-ideas-robert-manne"],", analysed the vicious antisemitism of ISIS. Accordingly, there is one eloquent passage in Schama with which I wholeheartedly agree:"]},{"k":"p","s":["Honestly, all we are asking for is to be left alone, to be able to send our children and grandchildren to a Jewish school in their uniforms, maybe with a kippah on their heads, without their being screamed at and being made to feel frightened and ashamed; to be able to ride public transport without being spat at or assaulted; to go to synagogue without having to pass through protective men with guns …"]},{"k":"p","s":["Schama believes he can help combat the rise of antisemitism in the diaspora by defending the indefensible: the war crimes of the IDF under Benjamin Netanyahu."]},{"k":"p","s":["I believe a more likely remedy would be for leading diaspora thinkers, like Schama, and influential publishers, like Morry Schwartz, and the leaders in Australia of the principal Israel and Jewish lobbies, like Jillian Segal, to speak the truth about what, under Netanyahu and the two ",["Kahanist","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Meir-Kahane"]," -fascist members of his Government, Israel has become."]},{"k":"p","s":["And about the moral revolution that has to happen in Israel if Jews in the diaspora are to be spared from the dreadful rise of antisemitism that is spreading around the Western world."]},{"k":"p","s":["This is an edited version of ",["an article originally published","https://robertmanne.substack.com/p/on-the-rise-of-antisemitism-in-australia"]," on Robert Manne’s Substack."]}],"chars":18886},"https://theconversation.com/how-north-korea-became-a-world-leader-in-state-sponsored-hacking-295597":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"27327d40d98cb12d8f25bd117b7c2724a0d6901629125b878a7c1fa2ea29a83f","read":"https://theconversation.com/how-north-korea-became-a-world-leader-in-state-sponsored-hacking-295597","bytes":183628,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"How North Korea became a world leader in state-sponsored hacking","by":[{"n":"Jinkyung Baek","r":"PhD Candidate in Politics Research, King's College London"}],"pub":"2026-11-03T08:52:18Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Binance, the world’s largest cryptocurrency exchange, was the victim of a massive heist in late October. Approximately US$6.36 billion (£4.74 billion) of funds were stolen within the space of 20 minutes in an attack that has been described as the largest of its kind in history."]},{"k":"p","s":["The source of the ",["attack","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/live/cqrmynd3042gt"]," has not yet been officially confirmed. But the ",["FBI","https://www.fbi.gov/investigate/cyber/alerts/2026/north-korea-responsible-for-6-billion-binance-attack"]," and blockchain analytics company ",["TRM Labs","https://www.trmlabs.com/resources/blog/north-korea-steals-usd-6-36-billion-from-binance-in-the-largest-hack-in-history"]," identified North Korea’s TraderTraitor hacking unit as the group most likely to have been behind it. Two other blockchain analytics firms, ",["Chainalysis","https://www.chainalysis.com/blog/binance-exploit-latest-findings-and-response/"]," and ",["Elliptic","https://www.elliptic.co/insights/binance-largest-hack-in-history/"],", have supported this attribution."]},{"k":"p","s":["This is not the first time ",["North Korea","https://theconversation.com/topics/north-korea-2060"]," has been linked to cyber-attacks. Several other cryptocurrency exchange thefts in recent years have been attributed to North Korean state-sponsored hackers. These include the ",["2019 Upbit","https://world.kbs.co.kr/service/news_view.htm?lang=e&Seq_Code=189232"]," and ",["2025 Bybit","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c2kgndwwd7lo"]," hacks, where funds worth US$42 million and US$1.5 billion respectively were stolen."]},{"k":"p","s":["Therefore, while Pyongyang has always denied any involvement in state-sponsored cyber warfare, it is not surprising that North Korea has once again been mentioned in connection with a major crypto heist."]},{"k":"h","s":["State-sponsored hacking"]},{"k":"p","s":["North Korea’s cyber operations are the product of decades of strategic development. The North Korean state began ",["building","https://www.38north.org/wp-content/uploads/pdf/2020-0228_SKA_NK-Cyber-Operations.pdf"]," the technological foundations for these operations in the 1980s, establishing ",["domestic computing infrastructure","https://www.38north.org/wp-content/uploads/pdf/2020-0228_SKA_NK-Cyber-Operations.pdf"],", ",["specialised educational institutions","https://web.archive.org/web/20040418233326/http:/www.nis.go.kr/eng/north/education20.html"]," and state-led research centres."]},{"k":"p","s":["The development of its cyber capabilities ",["intensified","https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm201719/cmselect/cmdfence/327/32707.htm"]," in the 1990s. The ",["Korea Computer Center","https://thediplomat.com/2022/07/mapping-major-milestones-in-the-evolution-of-north-koreas-cyber-program/"]," was set up in 1990 to oversee national IT development. And in 1995, North Korea’s then-leader, Kim Jong-il, ",["directed the military","https://www.38north.org/wp-content/uploads/pdf/2020-0228_SKA_NK-Cyber-Operations.pdf"]," to develop cyber capabilities as a strategic component of national defence."]},{"k":"p","s":["IT education was further institutionalised from 1998 onward after computer education was made compulsory for all students from the fourth year of middle school."]},{"k":"p","s":["North Korea began conducting major cyber-attacks in the 2000s. Early operations primarily targeted South Korea. But its cyber operations gradually expanded beyond the Korean peninsula, with the ",["2017 WannaCry attack","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-39919249"]," a prominent example of this development."]},{"k":"p","s":["This attack targeted computers running the Microsoft Windows operating system and ",["demanded ransom payments","https://techcrunch.com/2019/05/12/wannacry-two-years-on/"]," in the form of bitcoin cryptocurrency. It was ",["estimated to have","https://www.npr.org/sections/thetwo-way/2017/12/19/571854614/u-s-says-north-korea-directly-responsible-for-wannacry-ransomware-attack"]," affected more than 300,000 computers across 150 countries, causing ",["billions","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-us-canada-42407488"]," of US dollars in damage."]},{"k":"p","s":["North Korea’s state-led cyber operations start with the ",["selection of young students","https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2011/6/20/north-korea-recruits-hackers-at-school"]," who demonstrate outstanding academic performance, particularly in science and mathematics. These students are provided with specialised computer training at the elite Kumsong Middle School in Pyongyang."]},{"k":"p","s":["They then advance to one of ",["several colleges","https://www.chosun.com/english/national-en/2013/04/08/FQOCNI553NJT2I43EZDIKEI3TQ/"],", including Mirim College, Moranbong College, ",["Kim Il Sung University","https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2011/6/20/north-korea-recruits-hackers-at-school"]," or the Kim Chaek University of Technology. In these colleges, students ",["undergo training programmes","https://www.hrnk.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/RGB_Final.pdf"]," combining military studies with advanced cyber skills."]},{"k":"p","s":["As of 2024, North Korea was ",["estimated to have","https://www.donga.com/en/article/all/20240731/5091529/1"]," trained roughly 8,400 state-sponsored cyber personnel through these programmes. This figure is likely to have increased since then."]},{"k":"h","s":["Clear strategic goals"]},{"k":"p","s":["North Korea’s investment in cyber personnel training is driven by clear strategic goals. The main objectives of early North Korean cyber-attacks were to create social disruption in what Pyongyang sees as hostile states."]},{"k":"p","s":["Examples include the ",["2013 DarkSeoul attack","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-asia-23042334"]," and the 2014 cyber-attack on ",["Korea Hydro & Nuclear Power","https://www.piie.com/blogs/north-korea-witness-transformation/more-cyber-korea-hydro-and-nuclear-power-company-khnp"],". In the DarkSeoul attack, around 32,000 computers belonging to major South Korean broadcasting networks and financial institutions were disrupted by malware."]},{"k":"p","s":["The attack on Korea Hydro & Nuclear Power involved the theft and disclosure of personal employee information, nuclear power plant blueprints and technical documents. Both attacks aimed to disrupt critical systems and generate public anxiety. They allowed North Korea to exert pressure on its adversaries without engaging in direct military confrontation."]},{"k":"p","s":["In recent years, however, North Korea’s cyber operations have shifted towards making money. The more recent cyber operations are part of the country’s broader efforts to ",["maintain regime stability","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm5yn8eqg3npo"]," and fund its nuclear programme following years of international sanctions."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2024, a UN panel of experts ",["estimated","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/30/how-crypto-hacking-funds-isolated-north-korea-nuclear-programme"]," that the money stolen in North Korean cyber-attacks generated half of North Korea’s foreign currency income. One unnamed panel member added that stolen money funds “40% of the weapons of mass destruction programmes”."]},{"k":"p","s":["Several measures are in place to defend against North Korean cyber operations. These include financial sanctions and international efforts to disrupt money-laundering networks. Governments worldwide also ",["work closely with","https://www.secretservice.gov/newsroom/releases/2026/04/operation-atlantic-disrupts-more-45-million-cryptocurrency-fraud-freezes"]," the private sector to exchange intelligence, identify suspicious transactions and trace and recover stolen cryptocurrency."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the current measures have not been sufficient to prevent attacks. A key reason for this is the fact that international investigations and enforcement remain constrained by ",["national jurisdictions and sovereignty","https://sherloc.unodc.org/cld/pt/education/tertiary/cybercrime/module-7/key-issues/sovereignty-and-jurisdiction.html"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["As in the physical world, one country cannot access data or conduct an investigation within another country’s territory without its consent. Digital evidence can be also be quickly deleted or altered, so reliance on international cooperation can make an effective and ",["timely response difficult","https://www.europol.europa.eu/publications-events/publications/seizing-opportunity-five-recommendations-for-crypto-assets-related-crime-and-money-laundering#downloads"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Preventing North Korean cyber operations completely may be unrealistic. But there are several ways to make these activities less profitable and more difficult to carry out. These include strengthening identity verification procedures to prevent North Korean IT workers from gaining employment with false identities."]},{"k":"p","s":["They also include improving information-sharing between governments and private companies and enhancing cyber resilience. This would minimise the damage to targeted organisations and help them recover more quickly when attacks occur."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ultimately, the goal should not only be to prevent attacks, but also to disrupt the networks that enable them, limit the financial gains and reduce their impact."]}],"chars":6099},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/lithuania":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"20eb8367d03fe0043ff10f41325c55d7a23ac64301196c74596f4b8875e4c951","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/lithuania","bytes":71980,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Lithuania travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-04T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-11T10:19:26+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/lithuania/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Lithuania set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Lithuanian Embassy in the UK","https://uk.mfa.lt/uk/en/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Lithuania follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it might have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago, making it invalid for entry to the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can travel without a visa to the Schengen area, which includes Lithuania, for up to 90 days in any 180-day period. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["The requirements for ",["working in Lithuania","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/travel-to-lithuania-for-work"]," are different."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day visa-free limit. Visits to Schengen countries in the 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days. If you overstay the 90-day visa-free limit, you may be banned from entering Schengen countries for up to 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["At Lithuanian border control, you may need to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of your accommodation, for example, a hotel booking confirmation or proof of address for a second home"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of your travel insurance"]},{"k":"li","s":["show a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["prove that you have enough money for your stay – the amount varies depending on your accommodation"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, you must meet the Lithuanian government’s entry requirements. Check which type of visa or permit you need with the ",["Lithuanian Embassy in the UK","https://uk.mfa.lt/uk/en/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you stay in Lithuania with a residence permit or long-stay visa, this does not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read about ",["entry processes if you live in Lithuania","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-lithuania#passports-and-travel"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["New Schengen entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Union’s (EU) new Entry/Exit System (EES) is now being implemented across the Schengen area."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means that when you travel into the Schengen area for short stays, you may need to register your biometric details, such as fingerprints and a photo. There is no cost for EES registration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On your first visit into a Schengen country, you may be asked to register your details at a special booth before proceeding to the immigration desk. Follow directions from your travel operator or the staff at your port of entry. You may also need to provide either your fingerprint or photo when you leave the Schengen area. Children aged 11 or younger will not have their fingerprints scanned but can be required to have their photo taken."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to take any action before you arrive at the border on entry to the Schengen area, but EES may take each passenger extra time to complete so be prepared to wait longer than usual at the border and to allow more time for immigration controls when you depart the Schengen area. If you are departing the Schengen area by plane, allow extra time to reach your departure gate as EES checks may take place before boarding."]},{"k":"p","s":["EES is replacing the previous system of manually stamping passports when visitors arrive in the Schengen area for short stays. You may be asked to input biometric details every time you enter or exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Schengen area through the Port of Dover, Eurotunnel at Folkestone or Eurostar at St Pancras International, any information will be taken at the border before you leave the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your digital EES record is valid for 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are long-term resident in a Schengen country, you are exempt from EES in all Schengen countries. The full list of EES exemptions is on ",["the EU’s official Travel Europe website.","https://travel-europe.europa.eu/en/ees/to-whom-does-ees-not-apply"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If you believe you have been incorrectly entered into EES or your record of time spent in the Schengen area is incorrect, approach border officials when you next cross the Schengen border and request a correction. If you are exempt, carry documentation confirming your status. If you believe your time spent in the Schengen area has been recorded incorrectly, raise this with border officials and be prepared to provide supporting evidence if requested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Arriving from Belarus or Russia"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you arrive in Lithuania from Belarus or Russia, including the Kaliningrad Oblast, and need help, call +370 5246 2900 and select ‘calling about an emergency involving a British national’. You can also ",["contact FCDO online","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/lithuania/british-embassy-vilnius"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["British nationals can travel to Lithuania from Belarus or Russia by bus. Restrictions may apply if arriving in a privately owned car registered in Belarus. Russian plated vehicles are currently only permitted to enter Lithuania for up to 24 hours if transiting to or from the Kaliningrad Oblast."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information, see the ",["Lithuanian Customs Service website","https://lrmuitine.lt/web/guest/naujienos/aktualijos/aktualija?p_p_id=EXT_WPLISTALLNEWS&p_p_lifecycle=0&_EXT_WPLISTALLNEWS_obj_id=090004d2801f1006#en"],", and read FCDO’s ",["Russia travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/russia/returning-to-the-uk"]," or ",["Belarus travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/belarus"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Belarus-Lithuania border closures"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 29 October 2025, Lithuania announced the closure of its border with Belarus until at least 30 November 2025. However, UK nationals are exempt and can continue to cross the border. For further information on these restrictions, see the ",["Lithuanian Government website","https://vrm.lrv.lt/en/news/lithuanian-government-have-decided-to-close-salcininkai-and-medininkai-border-checkpoints-with-belarus-for-one-month-4zSU/"]," (only available in Lithuanian)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Only one border crossing point between Lithuania and Belarus remains open: Medininkai-Kamenny Log. Crossing the border is only possible by private vehicle; scheduled buses are currently not operating across the Belarusian-Lithuanian border."]},{"k":"p","s":["Lithuania also denies entry to cars registered in Belarus, with certain exemptions. For further information on these restrictions, see ",["the Lithuanian Government website","https://muitine.lrv.lt/lt/naujienos/lengvuju-automobiliu-su-baltarusiskais-registracijos-numeriais-ivaziavimas-i-lietuva/"]," (only available in Lithuanian)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Checkpoints on the Lithuania/Poland Border"]},{"k":"p","s":["Poland has reintroduced border checks on the Lithuania/Poland border. Vehicles may be stopped and documentation checked. For further information see ",["Poland Travel Advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/poland"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Lithuania guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/131/lithuania#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods that can be brought into and taken out of Lithuania","https://lrmuitine.lt/web/guest/verslui/apribojimai/bendra#en"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food into Lithuania"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them into EU countries","https://ec.europa.eu/food/animals/animalproducts/personal_imports_en"],". There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Lithuania"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques if the value is 10,000 euros or more. You will get a certified declaration to show you brought it in with you. If you do not, your money could be seized when you leave."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Russian vehicle restrictions"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are ",["restrictions on vehicles with Russian number plates","https://lrmuitine.lt/web/guest/naujienos/aktualijos/aktualija?p_p_id=EXT_WPLISTALLNEWS&p_p_lifecycle=0&_EXT_WPLISTALLNEWS_obj_id=090004d2801c0364#en"]," in Lithuania."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Lithuania"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Lithuania cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Petty crime is common. To reduce your risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["beware of pickpockets in busy locations and on public transport"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid unlit streets and parks at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["be cautious if you’re walking alone"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not leave coats and handbags unattended in bars, pubs and clubs"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink and food spiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been instances of drink spiking in Vilnius and at festivals and events elsewhere in Lithuania. Do not leave drinks unattended and be wary of accepting food and drink from strangers in bars, nightclubs and restaurants."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, are strictly prohibited, even in very small quantities. Severe penalties apply for possessing, using, buying, or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through airports. You should expect long jail sentences of up to 10 years and heavy fines."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Lithuania have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["While there are some LGBT+ friendly venues in larger cities, small towns and rural areas can be less accepting of LGBT+ travellers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex relationships are legal and same-sex partners can live together, but same-sex marriages and civil partnerships are not recognised under Lithuanian law."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Lithuania, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Lithuania guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/lithuania/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other requirements that you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Lithuania. Make sure it has the correct name and address. ",["Renew or update your licence","https://www.gov.uk/renew-driving-licence"]," if necessary."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience and a higher minimum age."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence in Lithuania, and the ",["legal limits are stricter than in the UK","https://policija.lrv.lt/en/road-traffic-safety/excerpts-from-the-lithuanian-road-traffic-rules/e-driving-under-the-influence-of-drugs/"],". If you are tested and have over the legal limit for alcohol in your system, you may face a heavy fine and imprisonment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check if you need a ",["UK sticker to drive your car outside the UK","https://www.gov.uk/displaying-number-plates/flags-identifiers-and-stickers"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is safer to use a recognised taxi app or to phone for a registered taxi that uses a meter. Make sure the meter is switched on to avoid arguments about the fare."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drone activity"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some Ukrainian drones aimed at Russia have crossed into Lithuanian airspace. Follow official guidance as necessary."]},{"k":"h","s":["Curonian Spit, Klaipeda"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Spit, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is divided between Lithuania and Russia – the northern half is in Lithuania and the southern half is part of Kaliningrad, Russia. Visitors should be aware that the walk along the Curonian Spit in Lithuanian territory will eventually reach Russian territory. See ",["Russia travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/russia"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 112 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Lithuania","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/131/lithuania#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Lithuania","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/131/lithuania"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Lithuania"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Lithuania","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/lithuania-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Lithuania, you need a Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or a European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"li","s":["private healthcare"]},{"k":"li","s":["private clinics"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Lithuania","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-lithuania#healthcare"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Lithuania"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 112 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Lithuania"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Lithuania"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Lithuania"]," in Lithuania"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Lithuania","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/lithuania-bereavement-information-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Lithuania","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/prisoner-pack-lithuania"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/lithuania/british-embassy-vilnius"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/lithuania/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Lithuania","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-lithuania"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":15858,"change":"Updated information on European Entry-Exit System (EES) (‘Entry requirements’ page)."}};
